+---
+id: dl-001-rental-module-conventions-half-open-periods-counter-booking-ids-integer-validation
+type: decision-log
+title: "Rental module conventions: half-open periods, counter booking ids, integer validation"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold, setting only `id`, `title` and
+     `status` (and any `--tags` or `--set` value). Fill the required frontmatter fields and replace
+     these placeholder comments yourself; `wingfoil memory submit` then checks the required fields,
+     moves `status` forward and commits the document as you left it, naming in the commit body the
+     content it carries. -->
+
+## Context
+
+The school's rules fix money in euro cents, UTC times, whole-day periods and no double booking. They
+leave open how overlap is defined, how booking ids are made and how input is validated.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A period is half-open `[start, end)`: a booking ending at midnight D does not conflict with one
+  starting at midnight D.
+- Booking ids are a per-service counter (`booking-1`, ...), not random or clock-based, so behaviour is
+  deterministic (project directive "determinism").
+- Prices must be non-negative safe integers; ids non-empty and unique; kind `board` or `sail`.
+- Times must be ISO-8601 with an offset and fall on midnight UTC; results are normalised to
+  `toISOString()` form. Total = number of days x daily price.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Back-to-back rentals work. Ids are unique only within one service instance, which suffices while storage
+is in memory; persistence will need a new id scheme. Cancellation will need to store booking start and
+total (already available in the booking).
